# Foundations Out of Sight

## Darwin’s Concession and the Long Test of the Subsidence Theory of Coral Reefs, 1837–1960

### Abstract

Charles Darwin’s theory that atolls and barrier reefs grow upward on sinking foundations is often told as a theory read from the shapes of reefs and confirmed, a century later, by the drill. This report argues that the shape of an atoll was never the evidence on which the theory stood, and that Darwin said so. From 1842 he conceded that a reef indistinguishable from an atoll could form on any foundation that happened to lie at the right depth, and he rested subsidence on an argument from number: so many foundations could not all lie at that depth by chance. His principal critics, Karl Semper, John Murray, Alexander Agassiz and Reginald Daly, worked within that concession and attacked the premise, each supplying a way in which suitable foundations could be common without subsidence. Because form could not decide the question, and because the surface evidence each side offered could be reread by the other, the dispute passed, on Darwin’s own proposal, to boring. The verdict that drilling returned was narrower than the theory. The Funafuti borings of 1896–98 did not reach a foundation; the Eniwetok holes of 1952 did, and established that one atoll rests on a sunken volcano. That vindicated Darwin’s inference about foundations while leaving his sequence of reef forms an open question, which later work has treated separately.

## 1. Introduction

In May 1881 Darwin wrote to Alexander Agassiz about the latest attack on his theory of coral reefs. He closed with a wish: {{q:L-borings}}.[^L-borings] Some years later a committee of the Royal Society bored into the atoll of Funafuti, under a preface that cited that letter as its warrant,[^FU-uncertain,FU-footnote|The preface’s footnote refers to the page of the letter quoted above.] and in 1960 two American geologists reported from Eniwetok, in the Marshall Islands: {{q:LS-abstract}}.[^LS-abstract] Read in sequence, these three moments make a familiar story: a theory inferred from the shapes of reefs, contested for a century, and finally confirmed when the drill reached the volcano beneath.

This report argues that the familiar story misplaces the evidence. The shape of an atoll was not what Darwin’s case rested on, and he said as much. What he conceded, what his critics did with the concession, and why the question could be settled only by looking beneath a reef explain both why the controversy lasted as long as it did and why its resolution was narrower than the theory it is usually said to have confirmed. The thesis has three parts. First, Darwin conceded in 1842 that a reef like an atoll could grow on any foundation at the proper depth, so that form alone could not show subsidence; his inference rested instead on the improbability that so many foundations should lie at that depth. Second, his principal critics attacked that premise, by supplying mechanisms that would make suitable foundations common without subsidence, and the surface evidence that either side offered could be reread by the other. Third, the dispute therefore moved, on Darwin’s own proposal, to what lay beneath an atoll, and the drilling that answered it decided the question of foundations without deciding the question of forms.

The argument rests on printed sources: Charles Lyell’s *Principles of Geology*, the abstract of Darwin’s paper of 1837, the first and third editions of Darwin’s book on reefs, two of his letters, the works of Karl Semper, John Murray, James Dwight Dana, Alexander Agassiz and Reginald Daly, the Royal Society’s report on Funafuti, the Geological Survey’s report on the Eniwetok holes, and two recent papers. It is a reading of these texts, not a survey of the whole literature, and its limits are stated where they bear on the argument.

## 2. Reading the foundation from the shape

The first explanation of the atoll took its shape as evidence of what lay beneath it. In the second volume of the *Principles of Geology*, published in 1832, Lyell put it this way: {{q:LY-crater}}.[^LY-crater] On this view the ring of reef repeated the ring of a crater: the foundation could be read from the form. Lyell anticipated the obvious objection, that many atolls were too large to be craters, and met it by appeal to the size of what were called craters of elevation.[^LY-size,LY-elevation]

Darwin’s paper read to the Geological Society on 31 May 1837, published as an abstract in the Society’s *Proceedings*, began by granting the crater theory its due. The abstract, written in the third person, reports Darwin’s view: {{q:D37-crater}}.[^D37-crater] What undid it was a second class of reef, the reef that encircles a high island at a distance. Of such reefs the abstract reports: {{q:D37-encircling}}.[^D37-encircling] A theory that explained atolls by the shape of their foundations had nothing to say about reefs whose foundations were plainly the slopes of ordinary islands.

In the book of 1842 Darwin pressed the objection from the side of form itself. The fifth chapter opens with the received view, {{q:D42-received}}, and asks at once {{q:D42-bow}}.[^D42-received,D42-bow] The shapes of atolls did not look like the shapes of craters. But the more important move in the chapter is not this objection. It is that Darwin stopped treating shape as evidence of foundation at all.

## 3. Darwin’s concession and the argument from number

Having objected to the crater theory, Darwin did not claim that an atoll could not rest on a crater. He wrote that {{q:D42-concession}}.[^D42-concession,D89-mnbf|Both this edition and the third print {{q:D89-mnbf}}. Read strictly, the phrase after {{q:D42-farfrom}} has one negative too many: the words that follow, {{q:D42-somesuch}}, show that Darwin is allowing that such a reef might be formed.] He made the same allowance for the rival theory of Chamisso, who had supposed that corals rising from a submarine base would reach the surface first at its edges and so form a ring: {{q:D42-bank}}.[^D42-chamisso-view,D42-bank] The allowance is a strong one. It grants that the characteristic form of an atoll, a ring of reef around a lagoon, could arise on a foundation that had never sunk. On Darwin’s own account, form did not distinguish the cases.

What did distinguish them, for Darwin, was number. Of Chamisso’s view he wrote in the same paragraph that a difficulty like the one facing the crater theory rendered it {{q:D42-inapplicable-short}}.[^D42-inapplicable] The difficulty is set out a few pages later: {{q:D42-foundation}}.[^D42-foundation] Across great areas of the Pacific and Indian Oceans in which no island rises higher than the waves can heap material,[^D42-spaces] it was not credible that so many submarine summits should all stand within a few fathoms of the surface and none above it; nor could elevation have brought them there, since that supposition was just as improbable.[^D42-improbable,D42-elevation] The conclusion followed: {{q:D42-necessity}}.[^D42-necessity] In a footnote Darwin turned the same reasoning against Chamisso: {{q:D42-chamisso}}.[^D42-chamisso]

The argument was not new in 1842. The abstract of 1837 already reports, among Darwin’s reasons for thinking general subsidence probable, {{q:D37-number}}.[^D37-number] What the book added was an explicit statement of what the argument conceded. Its structure matters, because the rest of the controversy turned on it. Subsidence was not observed beneath any atoll. It was inferred by excluding the alternatives, and the exclusion depended on a premise about frequency: that foundations at the proper depth, without subsidence, would be rare. Anyone who could show that such foundations were common would leave the form of the atoll exactly as it was and remove the reason for thinking that it had sunk.

Two qualifications keep this reading from claiming too much. First, Darwin did not rest everything on the argument from number. The 1837 abstract reports that {{q:D37-keeling}},[^D37-keeling] and the book proposed tests from the geographical distribution of reef types: whether the areas of atolls and barrier reefs stood in a determinate relation to those of fringing reefs and recent elevation, and whether recent volcanic action was connected with the movements so indicated. If such questions received answers in accordance with the theory, he wrote, then {{q:D42-confirm-short}}.[^D42-questions1,D42-questions2,D42-confirm] The distribution argument was an independent line of evidence, and this report does not examine Darwin’s map. But it too was an inference from the surface, and it could be met in the same way, by finding reef types together where the theory said they should not be. Second, Darwin knew that a section through an atoll would record its history. In a footnote on the same page he set out to consider {{q:D42-section-short}}, and described what such a section, formed during successive subsidences, ought to contain.[^D42-section,D42-ancient] In 1842 this was offered as a guide to recognising ancient reefs, not as a proposal to drill.

By the third edition of 1889 the concession stood, and the inference had hardened. The passage on craters now ends with the judgement that {{q:D89-incredible}},[^D89-incredible] and near the close of the chapter Darwin wrote: {{q:D89-compels}}.[^D89-compels] It was the need for foundations, not the form of the reefs, that compelled assent.

## 4. The critics take up the premise

The criticisms of the following decades can be read as a series of attacks on the premise about frequency, joined to a growing doubt that the structure of a reef could show anything about vertical movement at all.

Karl Semper, whose investigations in the Pelew Islands had preceded Darwin’s second edition,[^AG-semper,AG-untenable,L-mistake] made the second point directly. Arguing against Dana’s version of the theory, he reasoned that if local upheavals could occur within regions of general subsidence, and the reverse, then {{q:SE-structure}}.[^SE-structure,SE-ignore] On one atoll he found large dead blocks lying above high water, and judged that their position {{q:SE-blocks-short}}.[^SE-blocks] When Semper sent Darwin the proof-sheets of his account, Darwin replied in a letter later printed by his son.[^L-proofs] He admitted that he would have been troubled by what Semper found,[^L-troubled] and restated his concession in its sharpest form: {{q:L-foresaw}}.[^L-foresaw] He held to his conclusion for the central Pacific and Indian Oceans, but granted that {{q:L-future}}.[^L-adhere,L-future] The question, by Darwin’s own reckoning, had become one of frequency, to be settled by observations he could not himself make.

John Murray, one of the staff of the *Challenger* expedition,[^L-staff] attacked the premise head on. His paper to the Royal Society of Edinburgh in April 1880, published as an abstract, set out to show that {{q:MU-object}}.[^MU-semper,MU-object] Turning to Darwin’s own discussion of submerged banks, he observed: {{q:MU-improb}}. The deep-sea work of the *Challenger*, he argued, removed the improbability, since it showed {{q:MU-banks-short}}.[^MU-improb,MU-banks] The lagoon he explained by vigorous growth at the margin and the solution of dead coral within, and he concluded that subsidence was not needed to explain any characteristic feature of barrier reefs or atolls.[^MU-lagoon,MU-summary] Murray did not deny that atolls looked as Darwin described. He denied the frequency premise, on new evidence about the deep sea, and so removed the inference while leaving the form untouched.

Darwin saw the point at once. Writing to Agassiz in May 1881, he repeated the concession and the premise together, insisting that he could {{q:L-banks-short}}.[^L-banks] His reply to Murray was not an argument from the shape of reefs. It was a reassertion of the premise, now about the thickness of material that Murray’s organisms would have had to supply, and it ended with the wish for borings.

Dana, to whom Agassiz credited the general acceptance of the theory,[^AG-dana] answered the same challenge on the same ground. Of reports that reef rock in the Solomon Islands lay over limestone full of pelagic organisms he wrote: {{q:DA-local}}.[^DA-guppy,DA-local] He denied the frequency Murray needed: {{q:DA-free}}.[^DA-free] Of Agassiz’s view that atolls in the West Indies stood on a basement of pelagic limestone he wrote: {{q:DA-noproof}}.[^DA-tortugas,DA-noproof] Dana also offered evidence of another kind, which is the strongest counter-instance to the reading proposed here. In the deep, fiord-like bays of high islands within barrier reefs he saw {{q:DA-positive-short}}: drowned valleys, visible above water, recording that the land had sunk.[^DA-positive] Here, at least for barrier reefs, was surface evidence of subsidence that did not depend on frequency. It did not settle the matter. In 1915 Daly granted that some embayments might be explained in Dana’s way,[^DY-drowned] but argued from coastlines elsewhere for {{q:DY-rising-short}}.[^DY-rising] A drowned valley showed that the sea now stood higher against the land; by itself it did not show which of the two had moved. And for atolls, where no land remained above water, the evidence was not available at all.

Agassiz’s survey of the tropical Pacific, published in 1903, summed up the critics’ position: {{q:AG-opposed}}.[^AG-opposed] His alternative foundations were platforms cut by erosion and solution from elevated volcanic and limestone masses,[^AG-platform] and he turned Dana’s reasoning about local foundations back on him: where such rock bases occur, {{q:AG-reply}}.[^AG-reply] For the low atolls that mattered most to Darwin’s argument, the legend to Agassiz’s own reef chart conceded the decisive gap. It described them as low atolls whose land rims were of modern reef rock or conglomerate, {{q:AG-unknown-short}}.[^AG-unknown]

Daly’s glacial-control theory completed the series by supplying foundations at the right depth everywhere at once. Platforms cut by the waves while sea level was lowered in the Pleistocene were colonised by corals as the sea rose again.[^DY-depth,DY-colonize] In his summary: {{q:DY-crowns}}.[^DY-crowns] He stated the contrast plainly: {{q:DY-novertical}}.[^DY-novertical] Daly’s mechanism answered Darwin’s premise in its most general form, since a single cause brought many platforms to a common level within the reach of corals. He drew the corresponding conclusion about form: {{q:DY-deceptive}}.[^DY-deceptive]

It would misrepresent the period to suggest that the critics carried the day. Reviewing the controversy in an appendix to the third edition of Darwin’s book in 1889, T. G. Bonney judged it premature to regard the theory as disproved, and concluded that {{q:BO-insufficient}}.[^BO-premature,BO-insufficient] The point here is not who was winning but what the dispute had become. By the 1880s both sides accepted that the form of a reef did not reveal its foundation; they disagreed about how often suitable foundations could arise without subsidence; and each surface criterion offered in evidence, from raised blocks to drowned valleys, admitted a second reading. The question had moved out of sight, beneath the reef.

## 5. The test beneath the reef

Darwin had named the test in his letter of 1881. Agassiz named it too, at the end of the introduction to his survey: {{q:AG-boring}}.[^AG-boring] The Royal Society’s Coral Reef Committee, which the title page of its report lists with Bonney as chairman, framed its work at Funafuti in the same terms.[^FU-titlepage] Bonney’s preface to its report recalls the conviction that {{q:FU-uncertain}}.[^FU-uncertain] Edgeworth David, narrating the second expedition, opened his account of its results with that request: {{q:FU-asked}}.[^FU-asked] The work did not stop there; the preface records that {{q:FU-near}}.[^FU-near]

Funafuti met the letter of Darwin’s request and missed its purpose. The lowest part of the core had at first looked like the beginning of the original foundation, but proved on examination to be reef material converted to dolomite; David concluded: {{q:FU-david}}.[^FU-david-long] Without a foundation, the question whether one had sunk could be approached only indirectly, through the depths at which the organisms in the core had lived, and contributors to the report did approach it so. David and his colleagues noted that {{q:FU-pteropod}};[^FU-pteropod] J. W. Judd, estimating the rate at which the alga *Halimeda* was accumulating on the floor of the lagoon, concluded that {{q:FU-judd}}.[^FU-judd-rate,FU-judd] These inferences show that Funafuti was not silent on subsidence. But they were of the same indirect kind that had been in dispute for forty years, resting on assumed depths of growth and assumed rates of accumulation, and Bonney’s preface declined to draw them together: {{q:FU-facts}}.[^FU-facts]

The critics read the same rock differently. Agassiz held that below the modern reef rock the bore must have {{q:AG-funafuti}},[^AG-funafuti] and he proposed that the alternating beds of a reef flat, built up and cut down by the sea, might explain {{q:AG-layers-short}}.[^AG-reefflat,AG-layers] Daly argued that if a reef grows outward over its own talus as its foundation sinks, a bore near the rim would pass through talus rather than reef in place,[^DY-talus] and wrote: {{q:DY-site}}. The theory needed testing by bore-holes {{q:DY-inside}}.[^DY-site,DY-inside] He added a point against Darwin that a fair account must record: the boring at Bermuda, which he called the nearest approach to a vital test, did not favour subsidence, though he held that its value must remain subordinate until other borings were made in the Pacific and Indian Oceans.[^DY-bermuda] The Funafuti boring had produced more than a thousand feet of core that each party could read in its own favour, because it had not reached the one thing that all parties agreed would decide the case.

## 6. What Eniwetok decided

The deep holes at Eniwetok, drilled in connection with tests carried out in the Marshall Islands by the Atomic Energy Commission,[^LS-tests] reached it. Harry Ladd and Seymour Schlanger, reporting for the Geological Survey in 1960, stated the result without hedging: {{q:LS-established}}.[^LS-established] They counted this among the most important {{q:LS-features}}.[^LS-features] The character of the first sediments was decisive in exactly the sense that the earlier controversy required: {{q:LS-eocene}}.[^LS-eocene] Reef rock formed in shallow water, resting on a volcano that now lies more than four thousand feet down, is the observation for which Darwin’s argument from number had stood in. It is also an observation that pelagic banks built up from great depths, platforms eroded at sea level without later sinking, or benches cut by the Pleistocene sea would not lead one to expect.

Three features of the result keep it from being the vindication of the whole theory that it is often taken to be. First, it concerned one atoll. The drilling established the foundation of Eniwetok, not of atolls in general; Darwin’s argument had been about the many, and a drill reaches one at a time. Second, the history it revealed was not a steady sinking. Leached zones, land shells and pollen in the section showed that {{q:LS-emergence-short}}.[^LS-leach,LS-emergence] And the two deepest holes differed below 1,400 feet; the thick beds of the northwestern hole, the authors judged, {{q:LS-outer-short}}.[^LS-differ,LS-outer] Third, and most important for the thesis of this report, the drilling answered a question about foundations, not about forms. It showed that the Eniwetok volcano had sunk while reef grew on it. It did not show that the reef had passed through fringing and barrier stages on the way.

Recent work draws the same line. Blanchon and his colleagues, writing in 2014, put it directly: {{q:BL-insufficient}}.[^BL-insufficient] The drilling record, they add, shows that atolls {{q:BL-complex-short}}.[^BL-complex] Their own cores from Tahiti, where Darwin’s hypothesis first took shape,[^BL-tahiti] show that {{q:BL-retreat}}: a link between reef types, but by a mechanism of rising sea level that Darwin did not propose.[^BL-retreat] A review of atoll ecology published in 2024 gives Darwin the foundation and withholds the form: {{q:ST-correct}}; but {{q:ST-transient}}.[^ST-correct,ST-transient|The second passage cites a 2021 review by Droxler and Jorry, which is not examined here.]

## 7. Conclusion

The subsidence theory of coral reefs is often presented as a deduction from the shapes of reefs that waited a century for confirmation. The sources examined here support a different description. Darwin conceded that the shape of an atoll could not show what lay beneath it, and he rested subsidence on the improbability that so many foundations should lie at the proper depth. His critics did not refute his reading of reef forms; they attacked that premise, each with a new way of making suitable foundations common, while the surface evidence offered on either side proved open to a second reading. The controversy was long because its decisive object was out of sight, and it ended, for the question it could settle, when a drill reached a volcano under Eniwetok.

What it settled was narrower than the theory. Eniwetok vindicated Darwin’s inference about foundations, for one atoll, with an interrupted rather than a steady subsidence. It did not vindicate the sequence of reef forms, which the recent literature treats as a separate problem, reopened and in part answered by mechanisms tied to changes of sea level. On this reading the history of the controversy is less a story of a theory confirmed than of a question clarified: the drill showed which part of Darwin’s theory had depended on what could not be seen.

The argument has limits. It rests on a selection of printed sources and does not examine Darwin’s coloured map, Dana’s fuller treatment of distribution, the later borings that Daly and others called for, or the geological reviews on which the 2024 survey draws; any of these could qualify the claim that surface evidence was always open to a second reading. Two of its key texts, Darwin’s paper of 1837 and Murray’s of 1880, are available here only as published abstracts, the first in the third person. And it treats Funafuti as undecided on the question of its foundation, while acknowledging that several of its contributors drew inferences for subsidence from the organisms in the core.

